WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:08.160
Welcome back to the Deep Dive. I want to start today with a feeling. It's a specific kind of

00:08.160 --> 00:13.480
frustration that I think every single person listening to this has felt probably in the last

00:13.480 --> 00:18.420
24 hours. Absolutely. You're sitting there, maybe it's late, you're on your phone, you're trying to

00:18.420 --> 00:23.780
do something simple. Maybe you're trying to look up a recipe for, I don't know, chicken soup.

00:23.780 --> 00:32.040
Right. Or you're trying to find a human being to talk to at your bank and you hit this, this wall.

00:32.340 --> 00:37.560
The wall of frictionless friction. It's a great phrase. Exactly. It's supposed to be seamless,

00:37.800 --> 00:44.560
right? But instead you're scrolling past four screens of backstory about the chef's childhood

00:44.560 --> 00:51.340
in Tuscany just to find the ingredient list. And that list is sandwiched between two video ads

00:51.340 --> 00:56.880
that are slowing your browser to a crawl. Or you're caught in that loop with a chat bot that

00:56.880 --> 01:01.820
keeps asking you to rephrase your query. Yes. When you just want to know why you were charged twice

01:01.820 --> 01:08.500
for something. It feels like the internet, this tool that was supposed to be our library, our playground,

01:08.820 --> 01:15.760
our town square has changed. And I don't just mean the aesthetics, I mean the texture of it.

01:15.760 --> 01:21.960
It is a universal sentiment right now. I think we're all kind of grieving a vibe shift.

01:22.420 --> 01:28.060
A vibe shift. That's a good way to put it. We moved from an internet where you went to do things,

01:28.180 --> 01:34.260
to explore, to build, to connect on your own terms, to an internet where things are very much done to

01:34.260 --> 01:41.160
you. That's it. That's the feeling. We used to be explorers in this messy, open, digital wild west.

01:41.160 --> 01:46.800
It was exciting. It was. Now we feel like livestock. We are livestock in a very clean,

01:46.940 --> 01:52.940
very efficient, you know, very brightly lit digital factory farm. And that's not an accident. And for

01:52.940 --> 01:57.980
the longest time, whenever I felt this, the sense of being squeezed, I just chalked it up to standard

01:57.980 --> 02:03.540
corporate greed. You know, companies want more money, line goes up, quality goes down. A tale as old

02:03.540 --> 02:08.200
as time. Which is a fair assumption. I mean, greed is certainly a constant in human history.

02:08.200 --> 02:16.460
Right. But today we are diving into a source that says, no, stop. It's not just greed. It's not just

02:16.460 --> 02:24.020
bad CEOs. It says it's physics. Which sounds like a huge leap at first. But we are looking at a truly

02:24.020 --> 02:31.980
dense, really fascinating paper titled Optimization Without Restraint. It's by an author writing under

02:31.980 --> 02:38.940
the pseudonym Flixian published just this past February, 2026. Okay. And this isn't a light read.

02:39.040 --> 02:44.800
It's a structural analysis of the internet using the laws of thermodynamics. Thermodynamics, heat,

02:45.200 --> 02:50.660
energy, entropy, the stuff I barely passed in high school physics. Precisely. And the central thesis

02:50.660 --> 02:55.220
here is going to sound heavy, but we're going to break it down because it explains everything about

02:55.220 --> 03:00.300
that daily frustration you just described. Okay. Lay it on me. The argument is that the decay of the

03:00.300 --> 03:06.260
internet, the inshittification we all hate, isn't a moral failing. It is a thermodynamic

03:06.260 --> 03:11.660
inevitability. A thermodynamic inevitability. Wow. And it creates a framework to understand this,

03:11.720 --> 03:16.980
which the paper calls constraint-first ontology. Okay. Constraint-first ontology. That sounds like

03:16.980 --> 03:20.700
something I'd nod at during a dinner party while having absolutely no idea what it means.

03:21.040 --> 03:25.040
We'll get there. But before we get to the physics, let's set the scene a bit more.

03:25.040 --> 03:31.200
Because the paper spends some time contrasting the early web with the modern web.

03:31.320 --> 03:33.660
It does. And that contrast is critical.

03:33.960 --> 03:38.320
And I know we have listeners who might be too young to remember the early web, but for us,

03:39.260 --> 03:42.180
it was different, wasn't it? It felt completely different.

03:42.500 --> 03:49.120
It was radically different in its architecture. The paper uses the word extensible. The early web

03:49.120 --> 03:55.840
think late 90s, early 2000s was an environment of agency. It was messy. It was ugly.

03:55.840 --> 04:02.200
Oh, it was hideous. I mean, dancing baby GIFs, neon green text on black backgrounds.

04:02.840 --> 04:05.240
Angelfire. GeoCities pages.

04:05.800 --> 04:11.660
GeoCities pages that took 10 minutes to load over a dial-up modem. The sound of that modem.

04:11.660 --> 04:16.980
But it was yours. That's the key. If you didn't like how a webpage looked,

04:17.380 --> 04:21.660
your browser actually allowed you to change the fonts or turn off images entirely.

04:21.780 --> 04:23.260
Right. User style sheets.

04:23.400 --> 04:27.540
Exactly. If you didn't like your email client, you can switch to a different one and keep your address.

04:28.100 --> 04:31.820
The protocols, POP3, SMTP, HTML were open.

04:31.920 --> 04:32.960
They weren't owned by anyone.

04:33.300 --> 04:36.140
No. The architecture was designed to be reversible.

04:36.140 --> 04:42.720
If a service started acting against your interests, you had the structural power to just

04:42.720 --> 04:45.920
step around it. You could go somewhere else.

04:46.180 --> 04:50.620
I remember that. I remember feeling like a hacker just because I could change the background color

04:50.620 --> 04:53.140
of my MySpace profile. It felt like a toolkit.

04:53.360 --> 04:59.220
Exactly. The paper calls it participatory infrastructure. You were a participant. You were

04:59.220 --> 05:00.400
building it with everyone else.

05:00.460 --> 05:05.740
Okay. So that's the before picture. The messy, user-controlled, participatory web.

05:05.740 --> 05:11.520
Now compare that to the modern web. The paper describes our current state as

05:11.520 --> 05:14.120
extractive platform regimes.

05:14.320 --> 05:17.740
Which sounds like a government from a dystopian novel.

05:18.120 --> 05:24.640
It functions like one in many ways. The modern web is smooth. It's convenient. The friction is gone.

05:25.160 --> 05:27.180
Visually, at least, you don't have to code anything.

05:27.320 --> 05:29.240
You just sign up with your Google account and you're in.

05:29.480 --> 05:33.240
But every single interaction is designed to extract value.

05:33.240 --> 05:39.220
It's a casino where the exits are hidden, the clocks are removed, and the oxygen is pumped

05:39.220 --> 05:41.200
in to keep you awake and bedding.

05:41.700 --> 05:47.460
And if you try to change the font, you can't. If you try to use a different app to read your

05:47.460 --> 05:49.420
messages, you get banned.

05:49.620 --> 05:50.980
That is the core tension.

05:51.860 --> 05:57.120
We shifted from infrastructure we build together to these vertical towers of extraction.

05:57.300 --> 05:58.480
These walled gardens.

05:58.480 --> 06:04.640
And the hook of this paper, the thing that really grabbed me, is that Fliction claims

06:04.640 --> 06:12.100
this shift wasn't an accident. It wasn't just a series of bad decisions. It was mathematically

06:12.100 --> 06:16.400
inevitable once we removed certain constraints from the system.

06:16.780 --> 06:21.200
So we're going to get into the math and the physics, but first I want to push back a little

06:21.200 --> 06:23.020
because this isn't a new complaint.

06:23.160 --> 06:23.800
No, not at all.

06:23.800 --> 06:27.960
We have some really smart people who have already diagnosed this problem. We talk about

06:27.960 --> 06:33.820
Tim Wu. We talk about Cory Doctorow. The paper mentions them both. Why aren't their theories

06:33.820 --> 06:38.840
enough? Why do we need to bring thermodynamics into this? Isn't monopoly power a good enough

06:38.840 --> 06:41.180
explanation for why everything sucks now?

06:41.620 --> 06:46.100
It's a great question. And the paper gives a lot of credit to those existing frameworks.

06:46.220 --> 06:48.980
It's not dismissing them. It's building on them.

06:48.980 --> 06:55.920
So let's look at Tim Wu. He's the legal scholar who gave us the term net neutrality. His theory

06:55.920 --> 06:59.280
of extraction is basically the classic monopoly argument.

06:59.500 --> 06:59.620
Right.

06:59.880 --> 07:05.460
He says that when a company gets a bottleneck, when they control the only bridge into town,

07:05.560 --> 07:13.360
so to speak, they stop trying to create value. Instead, they just set up a toll booth and extract

07:13.360 --> 07:13.800
wealth.

07:13.800 --> 07:19.520
Which makes total sense. If Google is the only way to find things or Amazon is the only place

07:19.520 --> 07:24.400
to buy things, they can charge whatever they want for ads or for placement and show us whatever

07:24.400 --> 07:25.980
results make them the most money.

07:26.200 --> 07:27.520
Because where else are you going to go?

07:27.820 --> 07:31.000
We have nowhere else to go. That's the bottleneck.

07:31.120 --> 07:35.100
Right. And then you have Cory Doctorow, who coined the term in shittification.

07:35.360 --> 07:37.400
Best word of the decade. Hands down.

07:37.600 --> 07:43.740
It really is. It's so visceral. And Doctorow's model is very process oriented. He describes it

07:43.740 --> 07:45.600
this three-stage life cycle.

07:45.780 --> 07:46.920
Okay. What's the life cycle?

07:47.180 --> 07:52.440
Stage one. The platform is amazing to users. They subsidize you. They burn venture capital

07:52.440 --> 07:56.500
money to give you an incredible deal. Think of Uber rides costing $5.

07:56.940 --> 08:02.720
Or Amazon Prime being impossibly cheap for what you got. Free shipping, movies, everything.

08:03.240 --> 08:07.200
Or Google search, having almost no ads and just giving you great results.

08:07.560 --> 08:10.240
They do this to build a user base, to lock you in.

08:10.240 --> 08:16.000
The golden age. I remember when Netflix had every movie ever made for $8 a month.

08:16.100 --> 08:16.580
Exactly.

08:16.800 --> 08:17.040
Yeah.

08:17.260 --> 08:23.940
That was stage one. Then comes stage two. Once you, the user, are locked in, they start

08:23.940 --> 08:26.940
to abuse you to help their business customers.

08:27.160 --> 08:29.260
So the platform's focus shifts?

08:29.500 --> 08:34.940
It shifts. So Amazon starts showing you sponsored junk from sellers instead of the best product.

08:34.940 --> 08:39.420
Facebook starts showing you more ads from businesses in your feed than posts from your

08:39.420 --> 08:42.280
friends. They're now serving their business clients.

08:42.760 --> 08:43.660
And then what's stage three?

08:43.800 --> 08:48.080
Stage three. Once the business customers are locked in too, because that's where all the

08:48.080 --> 08:53.720
users are, the platform starts to abuse everyone to keep all the value for itself.

08:53.940 --> 08:55.100
Squeezing both sides.

08:55.380 --> 09:01.060
Yes. They raise prices for ads. They take a larger cut from sellers. They make the user experience

09:01.060 --> 09:06.960
even worse, ideally leaving just enough value so you don't delete the app, but taking everything

09:06.960 --> 09:07.220
else.

09:07.280 --> 09:10.440
The minimum viable product approach to keep you hooked.

09:10.860 --> 09:16.720
Precisely. Now, these theories are brilliant. They describe what is happening perfectly. But

09:16.720 --> 09:18.060
friction points at a gap.

09:18.320 --> 09:18.580
Yeah.

09:19.140 --> 09:21.420
These are descriptions of political economy.

09:21.560 --> 09:21.840
Okay.

09:21.840 --> 09:27.720
They explain the life cycle, but they don't explain the ontology. Why does this happen so

09:27.720 --> 09:33.260
reliably? Why does almost every system, whether it's a ride-sharing app, a search engine, a

09:33.260 --> 09:37.660
dating app, or a screening service, converge on this exact same behavior?

09:37.840 --> 09:42.680
You're saying it's too consistent to just be a series of bad decisions. It's like watching

09:42.680 --> 09:44.100
water always flow downhill.

09:44.440 --> 09:44.700
Yes.

09:44.900 --> 09:48.180
You don't blame the water for being wet. You look for the law of gravity.

09:48.360 --> 09:50.540
Precisely. That is the pivot this paper makes.

09:50.540 --> 09:57.980
It argues that we need to stop looking at tech companies as just businesses run by greedy

09:57.980 --> 10:01.880
people and start modeling them as gradient harvesting systems.

10:02.040 --> 10:06.420
Okay. Let's unpack this. Because when I hear gradient, I think of Photoshop. I think of a

10:06.420 --> 10:08.160
color fading from blue to red.

10:08.180 --> 10:09.180
Or a steep hill.

10:09.340 --> 10:12.760
Right. What is a gradient in the context of physics in the internet?

10:13.180 --> 10:18.140
In physics, a gradient is just a difference. A difference between two points. If you have

10:18.140 --> 10:22.660
a room where one side is freezing cold and the other side is boiling hot, you have a

10:22.660 --> 10:23.400
temperature gradient.

10:23.480 --> 10:24.780
Okay. A difference in temperature.

10:24.960 --> 10:32.480
And here is the fundamental law of the universe. Nature hates gradients. Nature wants equilibrium.

10:33.240 --> 10:37.480
It wants to mix the hot and cold until everything is lukewarm.

10:37.700 --> 10:39.260
It wants to even things out.

10:39.260 --> 10:47.620
Always. And that flow, the movement from hot to cold releases energy. That is how a steam engine

10:47.620 --> 10:53.100
works. You create a gradient and you harvest the energy released as it equalizes.

10:53.320 --> 10:55.660
Okay. So a waterfall is a gradient to them?

10:55.880 --> 11:01.260
A waterfall is a perfect example. It's a gradient of height. Waterfalls from high to low turns

11:01.260 --> 11:05.740
a turbine and creates electricity. You're harvesting the energy of that gradient.

11:06.180 --> 11:12.020
So a gradient is a source of power. A difference creates potential energy. How does Facebook or

11:12.020 --> 11:14.860
Google use a gradient? They aren't running on steam.

11:15.140 --> 11:18.080
The paper argues they run on informational gradients.

11:18.160 --> 11:19.100
Informational gradients.

11:19.160 --> 11:22.760
An informational gradient is the difference between what the platform knows about you

11:22.760 --> 11:24.700
and what you know about the platform.

11:25.240 --> 11:32.340
Oh, wow. Okay. So the hot air is their massive database of my entire life history, my location,

11:32.340 --> 11:33.980
my fears, my desires.

11:34.080 --> 11:34.560
With everything.

11:34.740 --> 11:41.560
And the cold air is me just trying to find a meme to send to my sister.

11:41.760 --> 11:46.020
That is the asymmetry. And this is where we need to talk about entropy. This is where people

11:46.020 --> 11:51.680
usually get lost in the physics. But the paper uses a card deck analogy that is really helpful.

11:51.860 --> 11:58.460
Please. Because I usually associate entropy with chaos or, you know, things falling apart. My

11:58.460 --> 12:03.320
messy bedroom has high entropy. That's one way to look at it. The classical thermodynamic way.

12:03.460 --> 12:07.680
Yeah. But in information theory, which is the math that underpins the internet,

12:08.540 --> 12:11.180
entropy equals uncertainty. Uncertainty. Okay.

12:11.300 --> 12:15.100
High entropy means I have no idea what is going to happen. It's unpredictable.

12:15.540 --> 12:19.000
Low entropy means I know exactly what is going to happen. It's predictable.

12:19.200 --> 12:23.860
Okay. High entropy equals surprise. Low entropy equals predictability. Got it.

12:24.140 --> 12:27.900
Exactly. Now imagine a deck of cards. If I hold a brand new shuffled deck

12:27.900 --> 12:34.380
and you have to guess the top card, your entropy, your uncertainty is high. You have a 1 in 52

12:34.380 --> 12:38.360
chance. You are guessing in the dark. Right. Maximum entropy for me.

12:38.680 --> 12:44.240
But what if I've been watching you play cards for 10 years? What if I've marked every card? What if

12:44.240 --> 12:48.760
I've built a file on you? And I know that when you're nervous, you tend to pick red cards.

12:49.420 --> 12:49.800
Okay.

12:49.800 --> 12:56.440
What if I know exactly how your hand twitches when you're bluffing? What if I have a camera watching

12:56.440 --> 13:03.180
your pupils dilate? My uncertainty about you and your next move drops to near zero.

13:03.180 --> 13:08.260
You've reduced your entropy. I've reduced the entropy of the system regarding your behavior.

13:08.720 --> 13:16.720
But my uncertainty about you, the dealer, is still sky high. I don't know the deck is rigged. I don't

13:16.720 --> 13:22.640
know what rules you're playing by. That is the gradient. The platform has near zero uncertainty

13:22.640 --> 13:28.060
about you. They know your credit score, your location, how long you hover over a photo of

13:28.060 --> 13:30.500
your ex, your debt load, your sleeping patterns.

13:30.580 --> 13:32.860
They have low entropy. They can predict me.

13:33.040 --> 13:37.420
But you have maximum uncertainty about them. You don't know how their algorithm works,

13:37.520 --> 13:42.280
what they do with your data, or why you're being shown a certain ad. You have high entropy.

13:42.280 --> 13:46.500
So that difference, that massive gap in knowledge, is potential energy.

13:46.680 --> 13:52.560
And they harvest that energy. They harvest it to extract value. Because they know exactly

13:52.560 --> 13:57.260
how desperate you are, or exactly how much you're willing to pay, or exactly what emotional

13:57.260 --> 14:02.260
trigger keeps you scrolling. They can manipulate the environment to get the result they want.

14:02.400 --> 14:07.900
So surveillance isn't just about spying in the sense that someone is watching you for fun.

14:08.180 --> 14:09.560
No, it's not voyeurist.

14:09.560 --> 14:13.500
It's about building a map of my weaknesses so they can run an engine off of it.

14:13.540 --> 14:13.940
Yes.

14:14.120 --> 14:14.460
Yes.

14:14.940 --> 14:20.720
Surveillance is the process of reducing their entropy about you. It converts uncertainty into

14:20.720 --> 14:21.320
leverage.

14:21.940 --> 14:24.160
And the paper has a name for this whole process, right?

14:24.160 --> 14:28.640
It calls it the vortex. It actually goes into the math in Appendix C using something called

14:28.640 --> 14:32.720
the RSVP Model Relativistic Scalar Vector Plenum.

14:33.140 --> 14:39.140
Okay, hold on. You cannot just drop relativistic scalar vector plenum into a conversation and keep

14:39.140 --> 14:39.640
walking.

14:39.860 --> 14:40.520
Laughs, I know.

14:40.600 --> 14:42.160
That sounds like something from Star Trek.

14:42.980 --> 14:49.460
Captain, the scalar vector plenum is collapsing. Break that down for us. What on earth is a plenum?

14:49.640 --> 14:50.000
Laughs.

14:51.460 --> 14:56.580
It does sound like technobabble, doesn't it? But the visual is actually quite intuitive once you

14:56.580 --> 15:04.280
get past the name. Plenum just means a space that is full of matter or structure, as opposed to a

15:04.280 --> 15:04.560
vacuum.

15:05.000 --> 15:05.260
Okay.

15:05.480 --> 15:09.880
So think of a rubber sheet, like the one you've probably seen used to demonstrate gravity in

15:09.880 --> 15:10.600
a science class.

15:10.660 --> 15:14.920
Right, the classic Einstein visual. You put a bowling ball on a rubber sheet and it creates

15:14.920 --> 15:15.340
a curve.

15:15.620 --> 15:20.940
Exactly. So in this model from the paper, the flat sheet represents scalers.

15:21.280 --> 15:21.720
Scalers.

15:21.720 --> 15:26.820
These are non-directional things. Think of them as the ambient environment. In a social

15:26.820 --> 15:32.340
network, this would be things like ambient trust, social norms, the general goodness of

15:32.340 --> 15:34.560
the community. It's the level playing field.

15:34.780 --> 15:36.100
Okay, the vibe of the place.

15:36.260 --> 15:42.080
The vibe, yes. Now imagine a vector. A vector is a force with a direction and a magnitude.

15:42.620 --> 15:45.880
In this case, the vector is profit maximization.

15:45.880 --> 15:49.640
The drive to make money. It was a very clear direction.

15:49.860 --> 15:54.900
And when you introduce an unbounded vector, a massive unrestrained profit motive onto that

15:54.900 --> 16:00.640
rubber sheet, it acts like a black hole. It punches a deep indentation into the sheet.

16:01.120 --> 16:02.880
That indentation is the vortex.

16:03.460 --> 16:08.720
And because it's a vortex, everything else on the sheet, the trust, the users, the content

16:08.720 --> 16:10.400
starts sliding down into it.

16:10.400 --> 16:18.660
Exactly. The vector dominates the scalers. The directional force of profit dominates the ambient

16:18.660 --> 16:25.060
non-directional force of trust. Everything swirls toward the center, toward the point of extraction.

16:25.280 --> 16:30.940
And the platform sits in the center of that vortex, harvesting the energy of everyone and

16:30.940 --> 16:32.840
everything swirling down the drain.

16:32.940 --> 16:33.600
That's the model.

16:33.820 --> 16:39.260
That is a terrifying image, but it makes so much sense. It explains why it feels like we're

16:39.260 --> 16:44.240
fighting a current all the time online. We're swimming against a gradient that was mathematically

16:44.240 --> 16:46.060
designed to pull us in.

16:46.260 --> 16:50.860
And this is an abstract. The paper details three specific manifestations where you feel

16:50.860 --> 16:54.800
this squeeze in your actual life. This is where the rubber meets the road.

16:54.940 --> 16:59.460
Right. This is where it stops being physics and starts being my crummy job or my ruined

16:59.460 --> 17:00.240
attention span.

17:00.240 --> 17:04.540
Exactly. And the first one, case study A, is about labor.

17:05.360 --> 17:06.780
Algorithmic wage discrimination.

17:06.780 --> 17:12.880
This is the one that really chills me. Because we usually think of a job market as, you know,

17:13.040 --> 17:16.900
here's a job. It pays $20 an hour. Do you want it? Yes or no?

17:17.160 --> 17:20.300
Right. The price is the price. It's for the job.

17:20.660 --> 17:27.340
But in a gradient harvesting system, the price is variable. It's not for the job. It's for you.

17:27.600 --> 17:28.120
What do you mean?

17:28.120 --> 17:35.580
The paper details how staffing platforms think gig work apps, but also automated HR systems for

17:35.580 --> 17:39.920
corporate jobs purchase data to map desperation gradients.

17:40.500 --> 17:46.380
Desperation gradients. That is the most dystopian phrase I have heard all year.

17:46.500 --> 17:50.120
It is remarkably precise, though. Let's real play this to make it concrete.

17:50.120 --> 17:55.060
Imagine you're a gig worker, a delivery driver. You log into the app.

17:55.120 --> 17:55.360
Okay.

17:55.520 --> 18:01.100
It's Tuesday. You have a $400 car payment due on Friday. If you miss it, you lose your car,

18:01.320 --> 18:03.400
which means you lose your ability to work.

18:03.500 --> 18:04.900
A very stressful situation.

18:05.220 --> 18:10.340
The app knows this. Maybe it has access to your connected bank account through an API you agreed to.

18:10.340 --> 18:16.760
Or maybe it has just bought credit data from a broker that shows you are over-leveraged and have no savings.

18:17.400 --> 18:22.560
So it knows I'm desperate. It has reduced its entropy about my financial state to zero.

18:22.940 --> 18:24.640
It knows my panic level.

18:24.760 --> 18:27.820
Correct. Now a job pops up. A delivery.

18:28.020 --> 18:32.120
In a fair market. Maybe that delivery job is worth $20.

18:33.440 --> 18:37.420
But the algorithm doesn't offer you $20. It runs a calculation.

18:37.420 --> 18:44.560
This specific human's error is desperate enough to accept $12.50 right now.

18:44.880 --> 18:46.260
And it offers me $12.50.

18:46.440 --> 18:47.980
It presents you that offer.

18:48.320 --> 18:53.080
And it might even add a UI element to increase the pressure, a countdown timer,

18:53.280 --> 18:57.720
or a flashing red notification that says your acceptance rate is dropping.

18:57.800 --> 18:59.240
It's manufacturing urgency.

18:59.380 --> 19:01.440
To exploit the gradient.

19:02.000 --> 19:04.380
So it's not paying you for the value of the work.

19:04.380 --> 19:11.140
It's paying you the absolute mathematical minimum it calculates you will tolerate before you starve or lose your car.

19:11.840 --> 19:16.400
It's personalized pricing for my own labor designed to give me the worst possible deal I'll accept.

19:16.620 --> 19:20.560
Exactly. It shifts from paying for value to paying based on vulnerability.

19:21.380 --> 19:23.540
It is extracting the surplus from your life.

19:24.220 --> 19:28.660
The platform's logic is, I know you need this money more than I need your labor right now.

19:28.700 --> 19:30.000
And it pockets the difference.

19:30.060 --> 19:30.860
That's the harvesting.

19:30.860 --> 19:33.220
That is harvesting the gradient.

19:33.560 --> 19:37.480
And because of the asymmetry, the entropy gap.

19:38.380 --> 19:42.960
I don't know that the guy sitting next to me in his car, who has a healthy savings account,

19:43.100 --> 19:45.820
was just offered $20 for the exact same job.

19:45.960 --> 19:46.980
You are in the dark.

19:47.380 --> 19:48.360
They are in the light.

19:49.260 --> 19:50.640
That is the power dynamic.

19:50.800 --> 19:52.880
And you said this isn't just for gig work.

19:52.880 --> 19:55.660
No, this logic is coming for every sector.

19:56.220 --> 20:04.920
If a company's HR system knows you just bought a house in an expensive area and had twins, it knows your risk tolerance is low.

20:05.040 --> 20:06.740
You're not going to walk away from a negotiation.

20:07.060 --> 20:08.060
You're less likely to.

20:08.420 --> 20:11.320
So they can squeeze your salary negotiations harder.

20:11.800 --> 20:15.440
They can lowball you because their model predicts you'll take it.

20:15.580 --> 20:16.400
That's horrifying.

20:16.400 --> 20:23.320
Okay, let's move to case study B because this is the one that affects literally everyone, not just workers in a specific field.

20:23.580 --> 20:24.440
The attention economy.

20:24.680 --> 20:28.080
Or as the paper reframes it, cognitive gradient harvesting.

20:28.760 --> 20:30.400
This reframe is crucial.

20:30.860 --> 20:35.200
We usually talk about engagement as if it's a neutral or even positive thing.

20:35.340 --> 20:36.900
Oh, I found an engaging video.

20:37.180 --> 20:38.380
I spent two hours on TikTok.

20:38.580 --> 20:39.480
It was so engaging.

20:39.640 --> 20:39.840
Right.

20:39.920 --> 20:41.000
Sounds like I was interested.

20:41.000 --> 20:48.160
But the paper argues that every scroll, every pause, every click is just a data point.

20:48.840 --> 20:51.860
It's reducing the system's uncertainty about your psychology.

20:52.360 --> 20:54.560
It is mapping your susceptibility.

20:54.820 --> 20:56.680
It's not figuring out what I like.

20:56.720 --> 20:58.980
It's figuring out what I can't resist.

20:59.200 --> 20:59.780
Exactly.

21:00.020 --> 21:01.860
It's figuring out your stuck states.

21:02.580 --> 21:07.480
What combination of stimuli will keep you paralyzed, scrolling, unable to look away?

21:07.620 --> 21:09.280
And this connects back to entropy, right?

21:09.280 --> 21:11.960
There was a concept you mentioned, entropy export.

21:12.360 --> 21:12.640
Yes.

21:12.760 --> 21:15.520
This is a fascinating thermodynamic concept.

21:16.420 --> 21:22.900
In physics, the second law of thermodynamics says you can't destroy entropy or disorder in a closed system.

21:23.280 --> 21:24.400
You can only move it.

21:24.640 --> 21:24.980
Okay.

21:25.120 --> 21:32.820
So to make your refrigerator cold inside, to make it a low entropy orderly environment, the machine has to work to pump the heat out the back.

21:32.820 --> 21:33.160
Yeah, right.

21:33.220 --> 21:35.040
The coils on the back of the fridge are hot.

21:35.040 --> 21:37.240
They're pumping heat disorder into your kitchen.

21:37.840 --> 21:43.480
To create order in one place, you must create an equal or greater amount of disorder in another.

21:43.600 --> 21:44.900
We export the chaos.

21:45.180 --> 21:45.380
Right.

21:45.660 --> 21:48.140
The platform wants to create order for itself.

21:48.740 --> 21:52.500
It wants a perfect, predictable, low entropy model of you.

21:52.860 --> 21:57.400
But to do that, it has to export disorder into your brain.

21:57.540 --> 21:58.420
How does it do that?

21:58.420 --> 21:59.780
It fragments your attention.

21:59.980 --> 22:01.400
It creates attentional overload.

22:01.520 --> 22:03.060
It hijacks your dopamine loops.

22:03.440 --> 22:08.040
So the platform gets smarter and more organized and I get more scattered and anxious.

22:08.440 --> 22:13.040
It's literally transferring the chaos from its servers into my head.

22:13.220 --> 22:15.440
That is the thermodynamic transfer.

22:15.820 --> 22:21.040
Think about the user interface of a modern feed like TikTok or Instagram Reels.

22:21.180 --> 22:23.160
It's not designed to satisfy you.

22:23.320 --> 22:27.020
No, because if I were satisfied, I'd close the app and go do something else.

22:27.020 --> 22:27.540
Exactly.

22:27.980 --> 22:31.880
It is designed to keep you in a state of unresolved anticipation.

22:32.840 --> 22:33.960
The slot machine mechanic.

22:34.160 --> 22:35.180
Variable rewards.

22:35.440 --> 22:36.040
Pull the lever.

22:36.140 --> 22:37.280
Maybe you get something great.

22:37.400 --> 22:38.400
Maybe you get junk.

22:38.600 --> 22:38.840
Yes.

22:39.520 --> 22:42.140
They sequence the content with surgical precision.

22:43.020 --> 22:45.480
They might show you three things that make you angry.

22:45.720 --> 22:47.020
Then one thing that makes you laugh.

22:47.300 --> 22:50.620
Then two things that make you feel inadequate about your body or your life.

22:50.800 --> 22:53.180
They're playing my neurotransmitters like a piano.

22:53.180 --> 23:00.860
They are finding the cracks in your psychology and prying them open to harvest the attention that leaks out.

23:01.160 --> 23:01.920
I feel that.

23:02.040 --> 23:03.860
I physically feel that fragmentation.

23:04.000 --> 23:05.560
It's like my brain is being mined.

23:05.680 --> 23:09.980
I wake up after an hour of scrolling and I feel empty.

23:10.400 --> 23:11.560
I feel drained.

23:11.660 --> 23:12.820
Because you have been drained.

23:12.960 --> 23:13.780
You have been harvested.

23:13.780 --> 23:22.560
The energy your cognitive focus was transferred to the system to reduce its uncertainty about how to manipulate you tomorrow.

23:22.760 --> 23:23.060
Wow.

23:23.280 --> 23:23.480
Okay.

23:23.540 --> 23:24.480
Let's go to the third one.

23:24.600 --> 23:25.480
Case study C.

23:25.700 --> 23:27.820
The collapse of informational trust.

23:28.300 --> 23:30.340
The Google sucks now phenomenon.

23:30.480 --> 23:30.780
Yes.

23:31.500 --> 23:33.280
The inshittification of search.

23:33.500 --> 23:39.680
I search for a product review and the first 10 links are just AI generated spam sites full of affiliate links.

23:39.680 --> 23:45.520
This is where we see this shift from ranking based on relevance to ranking based on yield.

23:45.840 --> 23:46.900
Yield meaning profit.

23:47.100 --> 23:47.680
Meaning profit.

23:48.660 --> 23:52.520
In the early web, a search engine had an epistemic contract with you.

23:52.720 --> 23:53.740
Epistemic contract?

23:54.320 --> 23:56.460
You're hitting me with the big words today.

23:56.760 --> 24:00.340
Lass, it just means a deal about truth about knowledge.

24:01.220 --> 24:08.040
The deal was, you ask a question and the search engine tries its best to give you the true, most relevant answer.

24:08.040 --> 24:08.680
Right.

24:08.900 --> 24:13.180
If I search best hiking boots, I want to know what the best hiking boots are.

24:13.700 --> 24:16.720
I don't want to know who paid the most to be at the top of the list.

24:16.800 --> 24:21.580
But in a gradient harvesting system, the search results page is no longer an answer.

24:22.080 --> 24:23.620
It's a revenue surface.

24:24.660 --> 24:29.360
The algorithm's primary question is not, what is the best answer?

24:29.600 --> 24:33.660
It's which ordering of these links will generate the most profit for us.

24:33.740 --> 24:34.180
Exactly.

24:34.180 --> 24:38.000
Sometimes that overlaps with the truth, but increasingly it doesn't.

24:38.360 --> 24:42.160
And the paper points out a key tactic here, the blended feed.

24:42.360 --> 24:43.180
The blended feed.

24:43.980 --> 24:45.400
This is what drives me crazy.

24:45.680 --> 24:52.900
They mix organic results with paid results, sponsored products, and their own services until you literally can't tell the difference.

24:53.040 --> 25:02.180
They change the sponsored tag from a big, obvious, bright yellow box to a tiny, faint gray text that is almost invisible against the background.

25:02.560 --> 25:03.860
It's the opacity again.

25:03.860 --> 25:05.660
It's about managing your entropy.

25:05.980 --> 25:11.860
If they had a big red flashing sign that said, this is an advertisement, the gradient wouldn't be as steep.

25:11.960 --> 25:12.700
Because I'd know.

25:12.880 --> 25:15.500
My entropy would be low about the nature of that link.

25:15.780 --> 25:16.420
I'd be skeptical.

25:17.040 --> 25:17.480
Exactly.

25:17.760 --> 25:17.920
Yeah.

25:18.060 --> 25:20.840
If you know it's an ad, you put up your mental defenses.

25:21.180 --> 25:22.520
You treat it with skepticism.

25:22.820 --> 25:29.680
But by blending it, by making it opaque, they keep you in a state of high entropy, high uncertainty.

25:30.040 --> 25:32.920
So they can steer your click to the highest bidder.

25:32.920 --> 25:37.900
They're exploiting the trust that was built up over decades in the search engine brand.

25:38.320 --> 25:40.560
The result is that we stop trusting anything.

25:41.240 --> 25:43.060
The epistemic contract is broken.

25:43.600 --> 25:44.820
The well is poisoned.

25:45.220 --> 25:51.820
Then the whole information ecosystem starts to collapse into a hall of mirrors where you can't tell what's real and what's just an ad.

25:51.820 --> 25:54.980
It all becomes optimized for yield, not for truth.

25:55.160 --> 25:56.740
So we have the diagnosis.

25:57.020 --> 25:57.720
We have the physics.

25:57.940 --> 25:59.980
We have the sheer terror of the vortex.

26:00.480 --> 26:04.420
But the big question for me is, how did we get here?

26:05.180 --> 26:08.060
Because the paper makes a big deal about constraint erosion.

26:08.360 --> 26:08.540
Yes.

26:08.660 --> 26:12.480
It says the internet wasn't always the specific type of hellscape.

26:12.620 --> 26:13.820
And don't just say smartphones.

26:13.820 --> 26:14.740
Smartphones.

26:14.860 --> 26:17.700
I feel like we always blame the iPhone for everything.

26:17.980 --> 26:18.940
It wasn't smartphones.

26:19.140 --> 26:19.900
Not directly.

26:20.400 --> 26:23.940
It was a shift in the legal and structural architecture of the internet.

26:24.980 --> 26:27.960
The paper argues that the early web had reversibility.

26:28.460 --> 26:30.220
This is a crucial concept.

26:30.900 --> 26:31.500
Reversibility.

26:31.800 --> 26:34.360
The ability to undo something.

26:34.360 --> 26:35.800
Or to change your mind.

26:36.880 --> 26:41.420
If a service got bad, let's say AOL started putting too many ads in its client.

26:42.240 --> 26:42.840
You could leave.

26:43.700 --> 26:46.640
But more importantly, you could modify your experience.

26:47.000 --> 26:48.660
You could use a third-party client.

26:48.740 --> 26:50.660
Agency was structurally encoded.

26:50.840 --> 26:51.120
Yes.

26:51.520 --> 26:52.840
It was baked into the system.

26:53.420 --> 26:55.160
But then we had a series of shifts.

26:55.620 --> 26:57.780
The paper calls it a fencing of the commons.

26:57.920 --> 26:58.160
Okay.

26:58.400 --> 26:59.320
What were the fences?

26:59.620 --> 27:01.320
First, legal shifts.

27:01.320 --> 27:08.420
We had anti-circumvention laws like the Digital Millennium Copyright Act, the DMCA in the U.S.

27:08.860 --> 27:12.280
Suddenly, it became illegal to modify the software you use.

27:12.600 --> 27:16.600
It became illegal to reverse engineer a protocol to make a better client.

27:17.240 --> 27:21.720
So if I wanted to build an app today that stripped all the ads out of the Instagram feed

27:21.720 --> 27:24.860
and just showed me my friends' photos in chronological order.

27:25.140 --> 27:28.540
You would get a cease and desist letter so fast your head would spin.

27:28.640 --> 27:30.480
And I'd probably get sued into oblivion.

27:30.480 --> 27:36.260
You would be sued, and you might even face criminal charges for circumventing a technological

27:36.260 --> 27:39.560
protection measure for breaking their digital lock.

27:39.960 --> 27:43.180
That is a massive constraint on my agency as a user.

27:43.440 --> 27:44.560
It's a huge fence.

27:45.120 --> 27:47.340
Then you have the expansion of intellectual property law.

27:47.800 --> 27:54.080
And then, crucially, you have the acquisition strategy, the kill zone, around big tech companies.

27:54.360 --> 27:58.160
Buying up any potential competitors before they can mature.

27:58.160 --> 28:06.460
Facebook buying Instagram and WhatsApp, Google buying YouTube and DoubleClick and Waze, Microsoft

28:06.460 --> 28:07.920
buying Activision.

28:08.380 --> 28:11.160
It prevents competition from ever getting a foothold.

28:11.560 --> 28:13.700
This created the lock-in we talked about.

28:13.860 --> 28:15.420
High exit costs.

28:15.580 --> 28:21.000
It's hard to leave Facebook because your grandma is there, and all your old photos are there,

28:21.120 --> 28:22.920
and your event invitations are there.

28:22.920 --> 28:25.160
The cost of leaving is too high.

28:25.600 --> 28:30.920
And once exit is effectively impossible, the platform doesn't need to fear you leaving.

28:31.460 --> 28:33.680
It removes all discipline from the market.

28:33.880 --> 28:35.300
They no longer have to serve you.

28:35.580 --> 28:36.520
You have to serve them.

28:36.740 --> 28:39.420
And this brings us to the mathematical heart of the paper.

28:39.860 --> 28:45.040
Section 4 mentions Appendix F, the Constraint Field Stability Theorem.

28:45.800 --> 28:47.020
Ah, the theorem.

28:47.020 --> 28:49.100
I'm going to need you to be very gentle with us here.

28:49.340 --> 28:51.440
What is the Constraint Field Stability Theorem?

28:51.840 --> 28:56.260
It sounds complex, but the idea behind it is actually quite intuitive.

28:57.440 --> 29:03.540
The theorem states that if you have a system whose primary goal is maximizing profit, that's

29:03.540 --> 29:04.580
the vector we talked about.

29:04.700 --> 29:05.920
The force with a direction.

29:06.180 --> 29:09.080
And you remove the constraints that would otherwise balance it.

29:09.520 --> 29:15.680
Things like competition, regulation, interoperability, the user's ability to leave.

29:15.680 --> 29:19.700
The system must mathematically converge toward extraction.

29:20.160 --> 29:21.440
It is an attractor state.

29:21.720 --> 29:22.840
An attractor state?

29:23.000 --> 29:23.680
Explain that.

29:23.720 --> 29:24.340
What does that mean?

29:24.520 --> 29:28.520
Think of a landscape with hills and valleys, like a big marble run.

29:28.800 --> 29:33.960
If you drop a ball anywhere on that landscape, it will eventually roll downhill and settle

29:33.960 --> 29:34.960
in the deepest valley.

29:35.160 --> 29:35.460
Okay.

29:35.580 --> 29:36.920
It's drawn there by gravity.

29:37.160 --> 29:38.720
That valley is the attractor state.

29:39.140 --> 29:41.720
It's the point where the system naturally wants to end up.

29:41.720 --> 29:45.760
The paper is saying, stop looking for the bad apple CEO.

29:46.200 --> 29:49.860
It's not about Tim Cook or Mark Zuckerberg being uniquely evil.

29:50.000 --> 29:51.040
It's about the landscape.

29:51.680 --> 29:53.100
It's about the shape of the landscape.

29:53.600 --> 29:59.800
If you build a system with these parameters, high lock-in, high surveillance, no interoperability,

30:00.280 --> 30:03.620
and a profit motive, it will always become extractive.

30:03.880 --> 30:05.860
It is the path of least resistance.

30:06.480 --> 30:08.680
The physics of the system demands it.

30:08.680 --> 30:13.600
So even if you put Mother Teresa in charge of a company with this structure...

30:13.600 --> 30:17.980
The structure would eventually force the company towards extraction, or the company

30:17.980 --> 30:21.920
would be out-competed by one that did extract, and it would fail.

30:22.580 --> 30:24.760
The vector of profit is relentless.

30:25.120 --> 30:28.500
If there are no laws to stop the ball, the ball rolls into the hole.

30:29.040 --> 30:29.840
Every single time.

30:29.900 --> 30:30.760
Every single time.

30:30.880 --> 30:34.960
That is both incredibly depressing and strangely liberating.

30:35.200 --> 30:35.660
How so?

30:35.660 --> 30:41.640
Depressing because it feels so inevitable, but liberating because it means we don't have

30:41.640 --> 30:44.000
to just hope companies become nicer.

30:44.340 --> 30:48.980
We don't have to rely on their benevolence or their corporate social responsibility statements.

30:49.360 --> 30:50.940
We just have to change the physics.

30:51.140 --> 30:52.900
We have to change the shape of the landscape.

30:53.460 --> 30:53.980
Exactly.

30:54.480 --> 30:57.360
And that is the pivot to the final part of the paper.

30:57.940 --> 30:58.540
The solution.

30:59.080 --> 31:00.420
The paper is very clear.

31:01.040 --> 31:03.900
We cannot just ask these companies to be nicer.

31:03.900 --> 31:07.100
That is like asking gravity to stop pulling.

31:07.640 --> 31:11.080
We must rebuild the invariant constraints.

31:11.440 --> 31:12.980
We need to put the fences back up.

31:13.180 --> 31:17.540
We need to build walls in the landscape to stop the ball from rolling into the extraction pit.

31:17.780 --> 31:18.600
Okay, let's build.

31:19.840 --> 31:23.020
The paper proposes a constraint-first solution.

31:23.380 --> 31:27.660
And the first remedy is something tech nerds talk about a lot, but maybe normal people don't

31:27.660 --> 31:28.800
really get the power of.

31:29.760 --> 31:30.280
Interoperability.

31:30.280 --> 31:31.280
Interoperability.

31:31.280 --> 31:31.680
Interoperability.

31:31.920 --> 31:36.060
What is it and why is it a magic bullet, as the paper calls it?

31:37.060 --> 31:40.640
Interoperability is the ability of different systems to talk to each other.

31:41.320 --> 31:43.680
It effectively pluralizes optimization.

31:44.340 --> 31:45.380
Pluralizes optimization.

31:46.080 --> 31:47.060
In English, please.

31:47.220 --> 31:48.200
Okay, think about email.

31:48.640 --> 31:51.660
Email is the classic example of an interoperable system.

31:51.740 --> 31:51.940
Right.

31:51.940 --> 31:54.780
Imagine if email worked like Facebook Messenger.

31:55.480 --> 31:59.520
If you had a Gmail account, you could only send emails to other people with Gmail.

32:00.220 --> 32:02.940
If you wanted to email someone on Yahoo, you couldn't.

32:03.060 --> 32:04.160
That would be insane.

32:04.260 --> 32:05.620
The whole system would be useless.

32:05.960 --> 32:07.760
It would be a collection of walled gardens.

32:08.480 --> 32:10.360
But email is interoperable.

32:10.900 --> 32:16.160
You can have a Gmail account and email someone on a private server or a Yahoo account or an Outlook

32:16.160 --> 32:16.540
account.

32:16.860 --> 32:17.880
It just works.

32:18.080 --> 32:19.360
The protocol is open.

32:19.640 --> 32:20.660
The protocol is open.

32:20.660 --> 32:24.440
The paper argues we need this for social media and other platforms.

32:24.920 --> 32:28.220
It's sometimes called adversarial interoperability.

32:28.820 --> 32:32.340
So if Facebook were interoperable, what would that look like?

32:32.660 --> 32:36.960
It would mean you could use a third-party app to view your Facebook feed.

32:37.440 --> 32:40.420
An app built by someone else, not by Facebook.

32:40.680 --> 32:41.820
And why would I do that?

32:42.140 --> 32:45.400
Because maybe that third-party app filters out all the ads.

32:45.540 --> 32:50.200
Maybe it sorts your news feed chronologically, showing you your actual friends first.

32:50.200 --> 32:53.340
Maybe it blocks all the rage-bait political content.

32:53.680 --> 32:58.820
So if the main Facebook app is full of tracking and algorithmic junk that makes me miserable,

32:59.580 --> 33:05.460
a developer could build a clean Facebook client that connects to the same data, but presents

33:05.460 --> 33:08.380
it in a way that serves me instead of Facebook's advertisers.

33:08.640 --> 33:09.180
Precisely.

33:09.320 --> 33:11.600
And here's the thermodynamic effect of that.

33:11.880 --> 33:13.080
It diffuses the gradient.

33:13.080 --> 33:17.680
The vortex can't form because the users have an escape hatch.

33:17.780 --> 33:18.260
An exit.

33:18.600 --> 33:23.780
If the platform tries to squeeze you too hard, harvest your attention too aggressively, you

33:23.780 --> 33:25.760
just switch to the client that doesn't do that.

33:25.760 --> 33:31.320
But it forces the platform to compete on quality, not on lock-in.

33:31.320 --> 33:34.780
It restores the reversibility we talked about from the early web.

33:34.780 --> 33:39.780
If I can leave the main app without losing my friends because my friends are still accessible

33:39.780 --> 33:43.900
through the new app, then the platform loses its leverage over me.

33:44.300 --> 33:46.440
It reintroduces discipline into the market.

33:46.980 --> 33:49.360
The platform has to be good or you leave.

33:49.960 --> 33:53.340
Right now, the platform can be bad and you are forced to stay.

33:53.340 --> 33:56.500
Okay, remedy number two connects directly to this.

33:56.940 --> 33:58.040
Data portability.

33:58.560 --> 34:01.820
Being able to take your digital life with you when you leave.

34:01.940 --> 34:02.180
Yes.

34:02.720 --> 34:05.020
This is another way to lower the exit cost.

34:05.640 --> 34:09.740
If you decide to leave Facebook for good, you should be able to download all your photos,

34:09.880 --> 34:14.380
your messages, your list of friends in a standardized format you can upload somewhere else.

34:14.520 --> 34:16.160
So you don't lose a decade of your life.

34:16.240 --> 34:17.280
You don't lose your history.

34:17.640 --> 34:18.800
And remedy number three.

34:19.880 --> 34:21.680
Auditability and bounded surveillance.

34:21.680 --> 34:25.920
This one sounds like it attacks the entropy problem directly.

34:26.400 --> 34:26.780
It does.

34:26.780 --> 34:29.760
This is about leveling the informational playing field.

34:30.400 --> 34:34.860
Bounded surveillance isn't just privacy settings where you click a box that says,

34:35.400 --> 34:38.580
I agree to a 50-page document you'll never read.

34:39.380 --> 34:43.740
It is a structural limit on how much data can be collected in the first place.

34:43.920 --> 34:44.160
Yeah.

34:44.160 --> 34:49.160
The paper suggests we need to introduce controlled noise into the system.

34:49.460 --> 34:52.640
Controlled noise, like adding static to a phone line.

34:52.840 --> 34:54.260
That's a great way to think about it.

34:54.840 --> 34:55.880
Informational static.

34:56.500 --> 35:01.800
Remember, the platform's power comes from seeing you perfectly while you can't see them.

35:01.800 --> 35:13.840
If we, as a society, legally limit the resolution of their data, if we make the picture of you a bit fuzzy, we prevent that informational asymmetry from getting too toxic.

35:14.060 --> 35:16.100
It's like camouflaging the user.

35:16.500 --> 35:18.960
You're still there, but you're harder to target.

35:18.960 --> 35:19.440
Exactly.

35:20.080 --> 35:21.480
The paper uses a hunting analogy.

35:22.000 --> 35:27.100
In many places, we have laws that say you can't use a spotlight or thermal imaging to hunt deer at night.

35:27.220 --> 35:28.640
Right, because it's not fair chase.

35:28.880 --> 35:30.160
It's not fair chase.

35:30.760 --> 35:32.780
The predator has too much of an advantage.

35:33.920 --> 35:39.240
Bounded surveillance is saying you cannot use a thermal imaging spotlight to hunt human attention.

35:39.240 --> 35:48.920
You can collect some data for the surface to function, but not enough to model our vulnerabilities with perfect, terrifying clarity.

35:49.160 --> 35:50.140
I love that analogy.

35:50.280 --> 35:51.300
It's about fair chase.

35:51.840 --> 36:03.500
And the paper wraps these ideas into a concept called entropy-bounded governance, treating data and attention as civic resources, not just private commodities.

36:03.500 --> 36:11.240
Yes. It's the idea that there is an upper limit to how much asymmetry is allowed in a democratic society.

36:11.720 --> 36:23.140
Just like we have environmental regulations that say you can't dump this much toxic waste into the river, we need information regulations that say you can't hold this much informational advantage over a human being.

36:23.240 --> 36:28.700
Because at a certain point, the leverage is just too high. It stops being a transaction and starts being a form of domination.

36:28.700 --> 36:35.700
It becomes exploitation. And the paper uses this to frame a vision for a better system, which it calls an operator ecology.

36:36.560 --> 36:37.240
Not a monarchy.

36:37.760 --> 36:45.080
Exactly. Instead of monarchs, these giant centralized platforms like Google or Meta, we want an ecology.

36:45.920 --> 36:49.820
Diverse, redundant, modular, interoperable systems.

36:50.040 --> 36:51.260
It's about resilience then.

36:51.820 --> 36:54.220
It's all about resilience over raw efficiency.

36:54.220 --> 36:58.860
A monarchy is very efficient in one sense, but it's brittle.

36:59.460 --> 37:01.820
If the king goes mad, everyone suffers.

37:02.340 --> 37:05.300
An ecology is messy, but it's resilient to shocks.

37:05.620 --> 37:09.600
And that leads us to the final philosophical horizon of the paper.

37:10.060 --> 37:13.560
Section 6 is titled, Against Maximal Optimization.

37:13.900 --> 37:14.120
Yeah.

37:14.120 --> 37:18.860
And this hit me because our whole society is obsessed with optimization.

37:19.360 --> 37:22.040
We optimize our sleep, our diets, our workflows.

37:22.460 --> 37:24.800
I track my sticks, my calories, my screen time.

37:24.900 --> 37:25.940
I'm trying to optimize everything.

37:25.940 --> 37:27.120
Like all life hacking.

37:27.360 --> 37:27.900
We are.

37:28.120 --> 37:30.500
But the paper challenges that very instinct.

37:30.640 --> 37:31.860
It asks the big question.

37:32.460 --> 37:35.760
Efficiency is always relative to an objective function.

37:36.280 --> 37:38.580
A system is efficient at doing something.

37:38.680 --> 37:38.820
Right.

37:38.820 --> 37:44.340
If the objective is profit, then efficiency equals maximum extraction.

37:45.340 --> 37:49.000
A perfectly efficient extractive system is a nightmare.

37:49.700 --> 37:54.760
The paper asks, should every second of human attention be a monetizable asset?

37:55.520 --> 37:58.940
Should every human vulnerability be a harvestable resource?

37:58.940 --> 38:00.220
That's the thing.

38:00.520 --> 38:09.020
If you build a machine designed to find and exploit vulnerability and you optimize it perfectly, you create a monster.

38:09.340 --> 38:09.700
You do.

38:10.160 --> 38:10.340
Yeah.

38:10.400 --> 38:16.800
You create a system that algorithmically finds the person with a gambling addiction and shows them casino ads.

38:17.140 --> 38:23.780
You find the person with the body image issues and flood their feed with diet pills and plastic surgery content.

38:24.660 --> 38:28.020
Maximal optimization, without any constraints, is cruelty.

38:28.020 --> 38:28.620
It is.

38:28.720 --> 38:31.140
It's cruelty at scale, automated, and efficient.

38:31.440 --> 38:32.780
And I think that's what we feel.

38:32.840 --> 38:35.240
That's the texture change we talked about at the very beginning.

38:35.700 --> 38:39.500
It's the feeling of a machine that knows us too well and doesn't care about us at all.

38:39.600 --> 38:42.320
It just sees us as a resource to be mine.

38:42.480 --> 38:45.220
And the provocation the paper leaves us with is stark.

38:45.860 --> 38:48.560
The choice isn't between technology and no technology.

38:48.920 --> 38:50.740
We aren't going back to smoke signals.

38:50.740 --> 39:00.040
The choice is between unbounded optimization, which inevitably leads to extraction and control, and constraint-aligned development.

39:00.300 --> 39:01.760
Which preserves freedom.

39:02.140 --> 39:04.220
It preserves the texture of life.

39:04.620 --> 39:08.380
It leaves room for the messiness, the unpredictability, the agency.

39:08.840 --> 39:14.340
It preserves the human element against the relentless thermodynamic pull of the machine.

39:14.500 --> 39:15.600
It allows for slack.

39:16.120 --> 39:16.640
Slack.

39:16.840 --> 39:17.780
That's the perfect word.

39:17.780 --> 39:18.860
Slack is inefficient.

39:19.340 --> 39:21.020
The slack is where humanity lives.

39:21.160 --> 39:22.620
It's where creativity happens.

39:22.940 --> 39:24.320
It's where grace is possible.

39:24.720 --> 39:29.860
So to recap our journey today, we started with that feeling of decay, the vibe shift of the internet.

39:30.200 --> 39:32.440
We learned that this isn't just bad vibes.

39:32.600 --> 39:34.640
It's the physics of gradient harvesting.

39:35.440 --> 39:44.420
Platforms reduce their entropy, their uncertainty to increase yours, creating a vortex that sucks value out of your labor, your attention, and your trust.

39:44.420 --> 39:47.540
And we learned that the root cause is constraint erosion.

39:48.100 --> 39:53.200
We removed the guardrails, interoperability, reversibility, limits on surveillance.

39:53.500 --> 39:55.180
That kept the system in check.

39:55.700 --> 40:03.540
And the constraint field stability theorem showed us that without those rails, extraction isn't just likely, it's mathematically inevitable.

40:04.000 --> 40:05.580
It's the attractor state of the system.

40:05.580 --> 40:06.860
But there is a way out.

40:07.080 --> 40:09.040
It's not just wishing for better CEOs.

40:09.860 --> 40:20.780
It's architectural reconstruction, interoperability, bounded surveillance, and changing the goal for maximal efficiency to a resilient ecology.

40:20.780 --> 40:23.940
It is an architectural correction, not a nostalgic wish.

40:24.120 --> 40:32.940
We can build a modern web that feels like the early web in spirit, open, free, and agentic, but with the scale and power of today's tech.

40:33.020 --> 40:36.280
But we have to choose to impose those constraints.

40:36.600 --> 40:39.620
We have to choose to be citizens, not just users.

40:40.300 --> 40:43.120
So here is the final thought for you listening right now.

40:43.120 --> 40:47.300
Reflect on this concept of optimization in your own life.

40:47.920 --> 40:51.340
Where has optimizing something actually made it brittle or less human?

40:51.980 --> 40:58.160
Maybe you optimized your schedule so much that one traffic jam ruins your entire day because there's no slack.

40:58.540 --> 41:03.080
Maybe you optimized your friendships via text so much that you never actually see anyone in person anymore.

41:03.440 --> 41:07.920
Where has the pursuit of efficiency eliminated the buffer, the humanity?

41:07.920 --> 41:12.680
Because what is true for the internet is often true for our own lives.

41:13.320 --> 41:19.820
When we treat ourselves like machines to be optimized, we start to extract value from ourselves, too.

41:20.800 --> 41:22.360
The paper closes with this sentiment.

41:22.700 --> 41:27.340
The choices between unbounded optimization and constraint-aligned development.

41:27.940 --> 41:30.820
One leads to a cage, the other leads to a home.

41:31.480 --> 41:32.660
Think about which one you're building.

41:33.060 --> 41:34.180
And which one you're living in.

41:34.180 --> 41:39.100
Thank you for joining us on this deep dive into the thermodynamics of the internet.

41:39.400 --> 41:42.900
It's been a heavy one, but hopefully you're looking at your screen a little differently now.

41:43.060 --> 41:43.900
See the gradients.

41:44.780 --> 41:45.680
Spot the vortex.

41:46.420 --> 41:47.440
Catch you on the next one.

